@page "/exchart/surface"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="3D surface"
          Lead="A surface over a grid of two axes, as a volatility surface over deltas and tenors. Turn it, zoom it, read a point, and click a row of the grid to draw its slice beside it as a chart.">

    <DocsSection Title="A surface">
        <p>
            <code>ExSurface</code> takes a <code>SurfaceDefinition</code>: an axis across, an axis along, and a
            value at each point of their grid, or none. Drag to turn it, or use the arrow keys; <kbd>Shift</kbd>
            with a drag or an arrow pans, and the wheel or <kbd>+</kbd> and <kbd>-</kbd> zoom. Move over it to read
            the grid point nearest the pointer. Click a tenor or a delta to draw that slice beside the surface,
            a smile or a term structure, as an ordinary chart. A point with no value is drawn as a hole, never as
            a zero: the surface below has no quote at 25C 5Y. "Reset view" returns it to where it started, and
            the SVG and PNG buttons save the drawing as it stands, turned and zoomed as the user left it.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Surface)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/VolSurface.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Interpolated by the page">
        <p>
            As a chart's curve is, a surface between quotes is the application's. ExChart.Engine's Interpolation
            Helpers interpolate a grid of quotes, bilinearly here, into a function the surface samples, so the
            page chooses the method and the surface says it: under the surface and in the tooltip, with no dot
            at an interpolated value. The helper does not reach past the quotes, so a cell with a missing corner
            stays a hole.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <MudLink Href="showcase/fx-volatility" Target="_blank">FX volatility showcase</MudLink> marks the
            values it filled instead: its grid of quotes keeps the quoted values, a spline across each expiry's
            smile fills a quote that did not come in, and the surface is handed the grid with those values marked,
            so its tooltip says "quoted" or "interpolated" at each point.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SurfaceInterpolated)" Mud="typeof(SurfaceInterpolatedMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/VolSurface.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="How it is drawn">
        <p>
            The surface is projected and drawn by the chart's own script while it is turned, because a round
            trip per frame cannot follow a drag. Blazor renders the toolbar, the tooltip, the slice and the table;
            the script reports the point under the pointer only when it moves onto another. "View as table" lists
            every value of the grid.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
